  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 327 AM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nA pattern change is expected going into the weekend, with a\ngradual warm up. The GFS and EPS ensembles are showing that a\nmid/upper-level ridge is expected to build over the Midwest into\nthe weekend, pushing the surface high and mid/upper level low to\nthe east. This would bring southerly flow back to the area,\npossibly increasing temperatures over the weekend into early\nnext week. There remains uncertainty in the exact magnitude of\nthe warm up however. NBM and LREF interquartile ranges for\nSaturday through Wednesday are around 5-6 degrees, so guidance\nis unsure if we will remain in the mid to upper 70s or heat up\ninto the mid 80s. These wide ranges are driven by a return of\nrain chances which could cool temperatures as low as the mid 60s\n(10% percentile). Long-range deterministic models are also\nhinting of a sharp shortwave moving through the central CONUS\nTuesday and Wednesday that could bring a cold front into the\nregion, but ensembles during the same time are having a\ndifficult time capturing the timing of the front with\ninterquartile ranges around 10 degrees by the middle of next\nweek. As a result, temperatures could remain in the mid to upper\n70s or drop back into the 60s to lower 70s.\n\nWith the approaching pattern change, our chances of rain\nincrease again. Friday into Sunday, deterministic models show a\nshortwave moving through the central Great Plains and into the\nMidwest providing support for a chance of rain Friday night\nthrough Saturday. The LREF indicates a 60-90% chance of\nmeasurable rainfall across central and northeastern MO over the\nweekend, but amounts are not expected to be significant with\nonly a 40-60% chance of 0.25 inches of rain in northeastern MO.\nThe possible sharp shortwave during the middle of the week also\nbrings the greatest chance of widespread rain through the\nforecast period. LREF guidance shows a 70-90% chance of 0.01\ninches of rain across our entire area, and 50-60% chance of\n0.50+ inches of rain.",
